                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{l_x}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{r_x}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; x
                    }
            %p commutes.
          %h3#in_terms_of_higher_monoidal_structure In terms of higher monoidal structure
          %p
            In terms of the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+n-categories"} k-tuply monoidal n-categories
            a braided monoidal category is a
            %em doubly monoidal 1-category
            \.
          %p
            Accordingly, by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            twice, it may be identified with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
            with a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            and a single 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
            \.
          %p
            However, unlike the definition of a monoidal category as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            with one object, this identification is not trivial; a doubly-degenerate tricategory is literally a category with two monoidal structures that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interchange+law"} interchange
            up to isomorphism. It requires the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton argument
            to deduce an equivalence with braided monoidal categories.
          %p
            A commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            is the same as a monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in the category of
            monoids. Similarly, a braided monoidal category is equivalent to a monoidal-category object (that is, a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonoid"} pseudomonoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://maths.mq.edu.au/~street/JS1.pdf"} 1986 paper by Joyal and Street
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+pseudomonoid"} braided pseudomonoid
            that specializes directly in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            to braided monoidal categories.)
          %p
            A braided monoidal category is equivalently a category that is equipped with the structure of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebra over
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+cubes+operad"} little 2-cubes operad
          %p Details are in example 1.2.4 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔼
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{E}[k]
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ek-Algebras"} Algebras
          %h3#the_2category_of_braided_monoidal_categories The 2-category of braided monoidal categories
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            BrMonCat with:
          %ul
            %li braided monoidal categories as objects,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+functor"} braided monoidal functors
              as morphisms,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+natural+transformation"} braided monoidal natural transformations
              as 2-morphisms.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
                is a braided monoidal category.
            %li
              %p
                The monoidal category
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒢ℳℴ𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{GMod}
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+modules"} graded modules
                over a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                (with the usual
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of graded modules
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                of the base ring. Infact, all braidings on
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒢ℳℴ𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{GMod}
                arise in this way (as in
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "JoyalStreet86"} Joyal, Street
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi B
                          %mrow
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi W
                          %mi u
                        %mo :
                        %mi V
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi W
                        %mo →
                        %mi W
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^u_{V,W} : V \otimes W \to W \otimes V
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi y
                      %mo ↦
                      %msup
                        %mi u
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      x \otimes y \mapsto u^{|x||y|} y \otimes x,
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |x|
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |y|
                denote the degrees. It’s evident that the resulting braided monoidal category is symmetric if and only if
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi u
